raman@cs.rochester.edu (Rajeev Raman) writes:
>I thought Andrew's posting was a suggestion for an enchancement to the
>game. Sounds like a fun addition to the game! Wearing the ring
>(unique, findable by killing a hobbit?), some chaotic creatures
>like Balrogs, Nazgul, wolves, wargs, orcs, trolls are tamed at
>once, the Necromancer "becomes aware of you" and comes after you,
>and if you wear it long enough, you turn (permanently) into a Nazgul.

I agree that it would be an interesting addition...yet it should be
real hard to actually get this ring...almost pure chance....perhaps
you should have to kill the 'friendly' invisible hobbit to get the
ring.  This has some nice effects of having your gods get angry with
you for going after the ring (by killing a friendly hobbit...the
hobbit hasn't been affected as much as golem has by the ring yet,
which is why he's friendly).  Putting on the ring would of course turn
you invisible and it would be sticky cursed (perhaps evil is a better
word).  For once you've gotten the ring off via remove curse or
what-not, when you put it back on, it'll be sticky again)  Perhaps
only a blessed scroll of remove curse would truly remove the curse
from the ring.  But, if the curse gets truly removed, it's magic is
destroyed and it becomes a normal trinket.  It would have many powers.
First, it would slowly change your alignment towards stringently
chaotic.  Once, it's made it this far, it could start slowly
polymorphing you into a Nazgul  (through a series of steps).
Through each polymorph your hunger for food decreases (need more food
to become satiated) and your greed for powerful weapons and gold
increases likewise.  Or perhaps, to continue, you need to drain life
energy from other monsters to supplement your nutritional
requirements.  If, once having the ring in your posession and on
your finger, you lose it, you will get a sense as to where its
position is.  If you drop the ring, it will 'disappear' from sight
(via teleporting to a different level).  Your sense of where the ring
is would only be 'above' or 'below' you when you go up or down stairs,
and the chances of you sensing it would be depending on how far your
relative distance is.  Also, the ring contains a certain plus luck
factor while it is in your posession...but should you drop and lose
it, you luck will immediately drop double whatever the plus factor
is.

	On the bad side, there will be certain monsters who will be
looking for you since you've found it (Ring wraiths and other baddies). 
The chances of them appearing would be a fraction of whatever it is
for regular wandering monsters, and as you get close to the
Necromancer, the number of baddies increases.

	The Necromancer could be like a cousin to the wizard...perhaps you
would have to kill him to get some item (like the ring, if you haven't
found it already, and something else) towards winning the game....


